A||||8||Scott2M4||Indication of a Cracked Head on 2.5||||5||
Z||000000||Scott2M4||01-15-2000||02:36 PM||||Will an engine show any symptoms if it has a cracked head (smoking, overheating, etc.)?||||reg||5
Z||000001||batboy||01-15-2000||03:43 PM||||Sometimes you'll get overheating. Sometimes you'll get coolant in the oil or oil in the coolant. Check the spark plugs, sometimes one will look lots different than the others. Sometimes you'll see white smoke (water vapor) in the exhaust.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Scott2M4||01-15-2000||07:53 PM||||batboy - thanks for responding.  Some of the conditions you describe sound like results of a head cracking internally.<P>What about externally?  Can a head crack externally and cause coolant to leak out onto the surface of the engine?||||reg||5
Z||000003||fiero56||01-15-2000||10:26 PM||||I'm not batboy(duh!), but coolant would only come out of a cracked head externally if you had a crack going from a water jacket all the way to the exterior wall. I know there is a picture on here somewhere of what the head to a v-6 looks like(with exposed water jackets). I'm assuming a 4-cyl isn't much different. I'll see if I can dig it up and post it here on this thread. A bad head gasket will also give very similar symptoms. Let me tell you about our x-windstar, that thing went through 3 head gaskets in 25,00 miles...<P><BR>I can't find it. The whole thread started when somebody mentioned rebuilding the their engine. Then they proceded to mention the engine was still in the car, and posted a picture of a 2.8 completely opened up, still mounted in the Fiero.<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 01-15-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||batboy||01-15-2000||10:37 PM||||It's possible to have a crack anywhere, but usually they crack in the exhaust valve seat area. Are you sure you don't have a hose or intake manifold leak? Coolant leaks can sometimes "travel" quite some distance before they became apparent.||||reg||1
Z||000005||batboy||01-15-2000||10:43 PM||||Unfortunately, the 2.5 head is prone to crack. Usually they're just minor stress cracks, but you still can't rebuild them. I had a crack in a Chevy small block head once and never knew it until I tore the engine down to rebuild it. A blown head gasket will sometimes leak coolant.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Scott2M4||01-17-2000||08:45 AM||||A (happy, I hope) ending to a nightmare ...<P>I put the car's rear end up on jacks this weekend to do some work underneath.  That's when I noticed the leak.  The coolant appeared to be pooling underneath the thermostat housing and, due to the angle of the car, trickled over the side (onto the starter).  When I tried soaking up the pool with a cloth, and it returned within a few seconds, I freaked out (this is when I jumped onto the forum).<P>Here's where (I hope) the happy ending comes.  When I finished the other work, and got the car off of the ramps, the leak went away.   ???????????????<P>Is it possible that, while filling the system at the thermostat housing, spilled coolant found its way to a recessed area on the engine and that tilting the car caused this "pool" to overflow?  Am I reaching?  Am I refusing to accept the fact that I have a car that's prone to cracked heads?<P>Guess I better start asking for suggestions/precautions on head replacement ... just in case!    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000007||fryecar||01-17-2000||09:40 AM||||I had the same thing happen to me last week on my 2.5.....  the problem turned out to be the thermostat housing. The gasket was a little tricky to find but Auto Zone here in our area had kind of generic one that worked.||||reg||1
Z||000008||batboy||01-17-2000||10:19 AM||||Remember I said earlier that coolant leaks can travel. I'd keep a close eye on the coolant level and look for more leaks for a while. It's possible what you saw was spilled antifreeze. Keep your fingers crossed.||||reg||1
